PRIVATE REFLECTION: {
The deliberation focuses on two primary Beatles songs featuring an opening modal jump from the major tonic to the minor fifth chord (I–v): "Strawberry Fields Forever" (A major to E minor) and "She's Leaving Home" (E major to B minor). While "Strawberry Fields Forever" introduces this harmony across its famous Mellotron intro and chorus, "She's Leaving Home" provides an explicit, unadorned root-position leap on its opening vocal phrase ("Wednesday morning at five o'clock... as the day begins..."). The group increasingly leans toward "She's Leaving Home" as the cleanest literal fit for a "chord jump," though both songs stand as the definitive musicological examples of this harmonic device.
}

